From a258691410665fe1ed3b605b4b59e41c2ca152ed Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Gavin John <gavinnjohn@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 16 Sep 2026 16:36:33 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 105/105] demux: ogg: end a group where the next one begins,
 not where the file does

Demux() reads every logical stream reaching its eos page as the end of a
chained group: the streams are torn down, the last of them parked in
p_old_stream, the pcr offset moved on to where the group ended, and
Ogg_FindLogicalStreams asked to pick the next group up from wherever the read
head now is. Nothing about that waits to see whether a next group exists, so
it runs at the plain end of every ogg file, and what then survives the seek
that follows - a repeat in place, or the bar dragged back after the track has
finished - is decided by whether the seek happens to leave the read head on a
beginning of stream page. It does not have to. test/samples/repeat/tone.ogg
is a second of vorbis whose setup header fills 2620 of its 3589 bytes, so the
first page carrying a granule falls outside the window OggBisectSearchByTime
probes before it bisects; the search settles on that page at 2620 rather than
on byte 0, Ogg_FindLogicalStreams reads a data page where it needs a bos page,
and the demuxer is left with no logical streams at all and hands nothing over
ever again. The same tone four seconds long, and the same tone in opus with
its 121 bytes of header, both reach a granule inside that window, settle on
byte 0 and get away with it - which is why this looked like a fixture quirk
and is really a coin toss.

A group ends where the next one begins and nowhere else, so read that page
before giving anything up. No page is the end of the file: report it and leave
the streams, their elementary streams, their headers and their index standing,
which is everything a seek back needs and more than it could be relied on to
find again. A page that begins no stream is no boundary either, and the loop
that clears the finished flag for pages after eos takes it. Only a bos page
takes the handover, which is then exactly what it was. Leaving the pcr offset
alone at the end of an unchained file also stops DEMUX_GET_TIME and
DEMUX_GET_POSITION marching an item length further on every pass; what the
handover did publish on its way past is still owed, since the eos page is read
with its stream already flagged finished and the pcr loop at the foot of
Demux() therefore leaves the last page of the group out, so Ogg_PublishLastPCR
does that wherever the group runs out, once, and hands the value back for the
chained case to pin the next group to.

test_src_input_demux_repeat goes from "tone.ogg: the repeated pass handed over
nothing at all" and "tone.opus: the repeated pass begins at 926501 us where the
first began at 1 us" to both rows passing, the other fourteen unchanged. Under
--aout=dummy --adummy-latency=50 --input-repeat=3, tone.ogg goes from 7916
samples for one pass and 7916 for four to 7916 and 32556, and tone.opus from
44452 and 189473 to 44452 and 177945 against 4 x 44452. loop4s.ogg, which
recovered by luck before, is unchanged at 175243 and 704672 and now gains
176476 samples per pass where it gained 176595, against an item of 176400.
tone.ogg and loop4s.ogg concatenated, which really is chained, still crosses
its boundary and reuses its elementary stream, and goes from 222942 samples
over four passes to 825323. DEMUX_GET_TIME reports 1000001 us at the end of
both passes of tone.ogg where the second used to report 2000001. Ogg no longer
carries its own timeline across an unchained repeat, so EsOutRepeatShift places
the passes instead - 3993, 7987 and 11981 ms on the four second file, with no
run-out and no shortfall.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
